The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2365 (2017), 2393 (2017), 2394 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018) and 2451 (2018),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Syria, in particular in the Idlib de-escalation area and in the north-west of the country, including the displacement of tens of thousands of civilians, the increasing number of civilian casualties, including children and women, and attacks against medical facilities, schools and other civilian infrastructure,
Recalling that the majority of those in need are women and children, and the need to ensure the protection of civilians,
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to the conflict to comply with international humanitarian law, including the obligation to respect and protect civilians and medical personnel, and to ensure the safe, unhindered and sustained access of humanitarian personnel and supplies to all those in need,
Recalling the ceasefire of 30 August 2019 and the need for all parties to implement it,
Welcoming the efforts of the Secretary-General and the Special Envoy for Syria to facilitate a political solution,
Determining that the situation in Syria constitutes a threat to international peace and security,
1. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities in the Idlib de-escalation area and fully implement the ceasefire of 30 August 2019;
2. Demands that all parties allow immediate, safe and unhindered access for humanitarian assistance to all areas in Syria, including through cross-border and cross-line deliveries;
3. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including with respect to the protection of civilians, medical personnel, hospitals, schools and other civilian infrastructure;
4. Demands the immediate release of all arbitrarily detained persons, and calls for the provision of information on the fate and whereabouts of all missing persons;
5. Emphasizes the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law;
6. Reaffirms its endorsement of the full implementation of resolution 2254 (2015) and the Geneva process;
7. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days;
8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2365 (2017), 2393 (2017), 2394 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018) and 2451 (2018),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Syria, in particular in the Idlib de-escalation area and in the north-west of the country, including the displacement of tens of thousands of civilians, the increasing number of civilian casualties, including children and women, and attacks against medical facilities, schools and other civilian infrastructure,
Recalling that the majority of those in need are women and children, and the need to ensure the protection of civilians,
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to the conflict to comply with international humanitarian law, including the obligation to respect and protect civilians and medical personnel, and to ensure the safe, unhindered and sustained access of humanitarian personnel and supplies to all those in need,
Recalling the ceasefire of 30 August 2019 and the need for all parties to implement it,
Reaffirming the need to combat by all means, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and international law, threats to international peace and security caused by terrorist acts, and stressing that the ceasefire shall not apply to military operations against individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaeda or other terrorist groups designated by the Security Council, provided such operations comply with international humanitarian law and human rights law,
Reaffirming that there can be no military solution to the conflict in Syria and that only an inclusive, Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process, facilitated by the United Nations, can bring about a lasting solution, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
Welcoming the efforts of the Secretary-General and the Special Envoy for Syria to facilitate a political solution,
Determining that the situation in Syria constitutes a threat to international peace and security,

1. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities in the Idlib de-escalation area and fully implement the ceasefire of 30 August 2019, without prejudice to military operations against individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaeda or other terrorist groups designated by the Security Council, including Hayat Tahrir al-Sham as an Al-Qaeda-linked group, and stresses that all such operations must be conducted in full compliance with international humanitarian law and human rights law, including the obligation to protect civilians and civilian objects;
2. Demands that all parties allow immediate, safe, sustained and unhindered access for humanitarian assistance to all areas in Syria, without undue restrictions, including through cross-line deliveries and cross-border deliveries authorized by the Security Council, in accordance with international humanitarian law, the guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance and relevant Security Council resolutions, and with full respect for the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic;
3. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including with respect to the protection of civilians, medical personnel, hospitals, schools and other civilian infrastructure;
4. Condemns all terrorist acts and reaffirms the need to combat by all means, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, threats to international peace and security caused by terrorist acts, and stresses that humanitarian assistance must not be diverted to terrorist groups, including Hayat Tahrir al-Sham and Al-Qaida-linked groups;
5. Demands the immediate release of all arbitrarily detained persons, and calls for the provision of information on the fate and whereabouts of all missing persons;
6. Emphasizes the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, including for the use of chemical weapons and by terrorist groups; recalls the need for full cooperation with the OPCW, including its Fact-Finding Mission and Investigation and Identification Team; calls for the full implementation of resolution 2118 (2013); underlines that States have the primary responsibility to investigate and prosecute such violations in accordance with their national legislation and international law; and calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with relevant accountability mechanisms, including the Independent International Commission of Inquiry and the International, Impartial and Independent Mechanism;
7. Reaffirms its endorsement of the full implementation of resolution 2254 (2015) and the Geneva process, stresses that the political process must be Syrian-led and Syrian-owned, facilitated by the United Nations, without external interference, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015), reaffirms the need to respect the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, emphasizes the importance of the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in the political process, and calls upon all Member States to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of Syria and to refrain from any unilateral action that could further destabilize the situation;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days, including on compliance with paragraphs 1 and 2, on humanitarian access and any impediments to such access caused by any party, and on obstacles to humanitarian access posed by terrorist groups and on measures taken to protect civilians from terrorist attacks;
9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

